          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            By a
            %em prefix order
            we mean a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} partial order
            which is also downwards
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+order"} totally ordered
          %p
            The concept was introduced in (
            %a{:href => "#Cuijpers13a"}> Cuijpers 13a
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Cuipers13b"} Cuijpers 13b
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantics
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/programming+languages"} programming languages
            %a{:href => "#Cuijpers13a"} Cuijpers 13a
            discovered that the generalization needed was something that expresses two things: time branches when it goes forward (due to different possible courses of action a system may take), but seems linear when looking backward.
          %p
            A different way of looking at it, is that executions of systems have a natural prefix order on them. Trying to come up with a generic definition of what a ‘prefix’ is, Cuijpers could not find anything in literature and came up with the definition below. As it turns out, in roughly the same period,
            %a{:href => "#FerlezCleavelandMarcus14"} Ferlez-Cleaveland-Marcus 14
            discovered the definition of “pointed prefix orders” and named them “generalized execution trees”.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#as_a_set_with_extra_structure As a set with extra structure
          %p
            A prefix order may be understood as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} extra structure
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %strong prefix order
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is a (binary)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq
            with the following properties:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+relation"}> reflexivity
                \:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq x
                always;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+relation"}> transitivity
                \: if
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi y
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi z
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq y \leq z
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi z
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq z
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antisymmetric+relation"}> antisymmetry
                \: if
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi y
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq y \leq x
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo =
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = y
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/downward+totality"}> downward totality
                \: if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq z
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi z
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \leq z
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq y
                or
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \leq x
          %p
            A
            %strong prefix ordered set
            is a set equipped with a prefix order.
          %h3#as_a_partial_order_with_downward_totality As a partial order with downward totality
          %p
            A prefix ordered set is precisely a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            satisfying the extra condition that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq z
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \leq z
            implies that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \leq y
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \leq x
          %h3#history_preserving_functions History preserving functions
          %p
            The morphisms of prefix ordered sets are
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/history+preserving+function"} history preserving function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in S
            %strong history
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/down+set"} down set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                    %mi y
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x{\downarrow} = \{ y \mid y \leq x \}
            %strong history preserving function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            from a prefix ordered set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            to a prefix ordered set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            (seen as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structured+sets"} structured sets
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x{\downarrow}) = f(x){\downarrow}
          %p
            Equivalently, it is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monotone+function"} monotone function
            between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            (interpreted as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posets"} posets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in S
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in T
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \leq f(x)
            there exists a
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x' \in S
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x' \leq x
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x') = y
          %p
            In this way, prefix ordered sets form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pfx"} Pfx
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+category"} concrete category
            over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pos"} Pos
            (rather than
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            %span.newWikiWord
              embedding open maps
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/embedding+open+maps"} ?
            as a generalization of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bisimulation"} bisimulation
          %h2#properties Properties
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Every prefix order is isomorphic to its set of histories under subsets, leading to the intuition that the elements of a prefix order ‘are’ their histories. Formally: given a prefix ordered set
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mo{:lspace => "mediummathspace", :rspace => "mediummathspace"} ∣
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H = \{ x{\downarrow} \mid x \in S \}
                is prefix ordered and isomorphic to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                (with ‘taking the history of an element’ and ‘taking the maximum of a history’ being the witnessing isomorphisms).
            %li
              %p Products of prefix orders are not the usual Cartesian products, but rather are formed by all possible ‘mergings’ of the histories of elements of the combined prefix orders.
            %li
              %p In the work of Cleaveland, a different type of products was defined, but at this point I do not know of a notion of morphism on prefix ordered sets that would result in that product.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li Be careful. Products of prefix orders are not the usual Cartesian products, rather the histories of a prefix order are ‘merged’. When taking the product of a countable and an uncountable history, unexpected phenomena may happen that leave one with an empty set. A ‘cure’ for this seems to be to restrict to prefix orders in which each history has a minimum.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} partial order
            %li
              In some books on order theory, a partially ordered set that is downward well-ordered is used as a characterization of a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tree"} tree
            %li
              Any partial order can be viewed as an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexandroff+topology"} Alexandroff topology
          %h2#References References
          %ul
            %li#Cuijpers13a
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pieter+Cuijpers"}> Pieter Cuijpers
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Prefix Orders as a General Model of Dynamics
                %a{:href => "http://www.dcm-workshop.org.uk/2013/"} 9th International Workshop on Developments in Computational Models (DCM)
                p. 25–29 (
                %a{:href => "http://www.dcm-workshop.org.uk/2013/dcm-2013-pre-proc.pdf#page=30"}> pdf
                )
            %li#Cuijpers13b
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pieter+Cuijpers"}> Pieter Cuijpers
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Categorical Limit of a Sequence of Dynamical Systems
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.win.tue.nl/expresssos2013/"} EPTCS 120 : Proceedings EXPRESS/SOS 2013
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.7445"} arXiv:1307.7445
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => ""} doi:10.4204/EPTCS.120.7
            %li#FerlezCleavelandMarcus14
              %p
                James Ferlez, Rance Cleaveland, Steve Marcus,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Generalized Synchronization Trees
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-54830-7_20"} 10.1007/978-3-642-54830-7_20
        .revisedby
